      "id": "verbosity_explain_water",
      "category": "verbosity_fingerprint",
      "check_type": "length_range",
      "passed": false,
      "errored": false,
      "error": null,
      "latency_ms": 2725.7,
      "prompt_tokens": 11,
      "completion_tokens": 638,
      "output": "At its most fundamental level, **water** is a chemical compound made up of two types of atoms: **hydrogen** and **oxygen**. Its chemical formula is **$H_2O$**—meaning every single molecule of water consists of two hydrogen atoms bonded to one oxygen atom.\n\nHowever, water is much more than just a chemical formula. It is the most abundant and arguably the most important substance on Earth for sustaining life. \n\nHere is a breakdown of what water is, looking at it through different lenses:\n\n---\n\n### 1. Physical Properties (The Science)\nWater is unique because it is the only natural substance on Earth that exists abundantly in three physical states:\n*   **Liquid:** The standard form we drink, swim in, and rain from the sky.\n*   **Solid (Ice):** Frozen water. Uniquely, solid water is *less dense* than liquid water, which is why ice floats. If ice sank, oceans would freeze from the bottom up, destroying aquatic life.\n*   **Gas (Vapor/Steam):** Water in the form of an invisible gas in the atmosphere.\n\n**Other key properties:**\n*   **The \"Universal Solvent\":** Water can dissolve more substances (like salt, sugar, and minerals) than any other liquid. This is how our bodies absorb nutrients and how oceans stay salty.\n*   **High Heat Capacity:** Water can absorb and release large amounts of heat. This helps regulate the climate of the Earth and keeps human body temperatures stable.\n*   **Cohesion and Adhesion:** Water molecules like to stick to each other (cohesion—creating surface tension, allowing insects to walk on water) and to other surfaces (adhesion—allowing water to travel up the roots of tall trees).\n\n---\n\n### 2. Ecological Role (On Earth)\nWater covers about **71% of the Earth's surface**, mostly in oceans and seas (which hold about 96.5% of all Earth's water). \n*   **The Water Cycle:** Water is endlessly recycled through evaporation, condensation, precipitation (rain/snow), and collection. The water you drink today might have been swallowed by a dinosaur millions of years ago.\n*   **Climate Regulation:** Oceans absorb heat from the sun and distribute it around the globe via currents, making the planet habitable.\n\n---\n\n### 3. Biological Role (For Life)\nLife as we know it cannot exist without water. \n*   **Human Body:** The adult human body is made up of roughly **60% water**. The brain is about 73% water, and the lungs are about 83%.\n*   **Function:** Water acts as a lubricant for joints, flushes out waste through urine and sweat, regulates body temperature, and serves as the medium in which all cellular metabolic reactions take place.\n\n---\n\n### Summary\nWater is the lifeblood of planet Earth. It is a simple molecule with extraordinary, life-enabling properties that shapes our geology, dictates our weather, and makes biological existence possible."
